From a £50.00 peak to £35.00 is 15 / 50 = 0.3. One correct version computes `1 - p / peak`, which gives 0.30000000000000004, so `==` would fail it. The steady rise catches the range bug, and a peak after the first price catches the first-price bug.

```python
def test_one_dip():
    assert max_drawdown([40.00, 50.00, 35.00, 45.00]) == pytest.approx(0.3)

def test_flat():
    assert max_drawdown([100.00, 100.00, 100.00]) == pytest.approx(0.0)

def test_steady_rise():
    assert max_drawdown([100.00, 105.00, 120.00]) == pytest.approx(0.0)
